# TLS in front, because the phone will not talk to it otherwise.
#
# iOS App Transport Security refuses plain HTTP from an app, so the
# registration call needs a certificate — and a certificate needs a name. Caddy
# gets one from Let's Encrypt on first start and renews it without being asked.
#
# push.cubechat.tech is the real name and the one the app asks for first. It
# needs one A record pointing here; the apex stays on GitHub Pages for the
# marketing site and is not touched.
#
# The sslip.io block stays until no shipped build is still asking for it. It
# was the way in while cubechat.qpon sat undelegated in the .qpon registry —
# sslip.io resolves any `a-b-c-d.sslip.io` to the address written in the name —
# and that same trick is what DNS rebinding protection blocks, which is the
# reason it is no longer the first choice. See `push_registration.dart`.
#
# cubechat.qpon is kept out: it has never resolved, so Caddy would fail the
# ACME challenge for it on every start.

# /admin/* (Task S2) is for the Telegram bot on this droplet, talking to
# :8080 directly — it must never be reachable from the internet at all. The
# Node handler already refuses it without a loopback remoteAddress and a
# bearer token, but Caddy proxying a stranger's request to 127.0.0.1 is
# exactly what makes that remoteAddress check alone insufficient (see the
# comment above handleAdmin in push/src/index.js). So it's blocked here too,
# before reverse_proxy ever sees it — belt and suspenders, not either alone.

push.cubechat.tech {
	@admin path /admin/*
	respond @admin 404

	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}

209-38-225-225.sslip.io {
	@admin path /admin/*
	respond @admin 404

	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}

# Our own Nostr relay (strfry, see relay/), which carries the media lane.
#
# **This block must live in this file.** It used to be appended to the live
# /etc/caddy/Caddyfile by hand and existed nowhere in the repo, so the moderation
# deploy on 2026-09-24 copied this file over the live one — as the deploy README
# says to — and relay.cubechat.tech lost its certificate: every phone's relay
# connection failed with TLSV1_ALERT_INTERNAL_ERROR until it was restored the
# next morning. This file is the whole live config; keep it that way.
#
# A relay is a WebSocket and Caddy's reverse_proxy upgrades one without being
# told to, so there is nothing here about Connection or Upgrade headers.
#
# **keepalive off, and that is not a preference.** strfry's HTTP side is the
# small server built into uWebSockets, and it does not hold a connection the
# way Caddy's pool expects. With keep-alive on, every plain HTTP request came
# back to Caddy as an "Unsolicited response received on idle HTTP channel" and
# was thrown away — so `curl https://relay.cubechat.tech` returned nothing at
# all while the relay behind it was answering perfectly. The WebSocket upgrade
# is a fresh connection either way, so nothing about the actual traffic pays
# for this.
#
# X-Forwarded-For is not set here either: Caddy passes it by default and says
# so out loud when told to do it twice.
relay.cubechat.tech {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8081 {
		transport http {
			versions 1.1
			keepalive off
		}
	}
}
